\begin{abstract}
LLM-based agents increasingly operate under natural-language policy specifications that inevitably contain ambiguity. We present a matched-pair causal study on $\tau^2$-bench with 300 ambiguous/unambiguous clause pairs spanning a six-type taxonomy, evaluated across five models (2,997 episodes). Ambiguous clauses are consistently nocuous (i.e., harmful), increasing violation rates by 34.0 percentage points (conditional OR $= 15.1$, matched-pair), with perturbation controls confirming specificity to semantic ambiguity. A variance decomposition reveals that individual clause wording explains 22.4\% of violation variance versus 0.4\% for ambiguity type and 1.0\% for model identity, establishing clause-level wording as the dominant risk factor. However, a normalized mutual information analysis shows that type membership predicts failure \emph{mechanism} 17$\times$ more strongly than it predicts violation rate, establishing the taxonomy as a diagnostic tool for failure-mechanism prediction rather than risk prediction. Per-type effects form a partial ordering with robust extremes: incompleteness as the consistent upper bound ($+$49.2\,pp, conditional on an information-addition confound) and coreferential as the lower bound ($+$18.7\,pp), and a margin-dependent mid-tier structure spanning $+$30.8 to $+$36.0\,pp. Error analysis of 614 violation traces reveals \emph{Disambiguation Neglect} ($\leq$11\% per-model awareness) and \emph{systematic nocuity}: 41.3\% of clauses triggering model-convergent violations. These results, observed in customer-service policy domains, motivate clause-level policy auditing and type-targeted diagnosis for policy-governed deployments.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

A customer asks to return a customized item within the 30-day window. The agent consults the store policy, which states that ``items may be returned within 30 days.'' The word ``items'' scopes over all product categories, including custom orders that are normally final sale. The agent grants a full refund and arranges free return shipping. One word of scopal ambiguity costs the retailer \$200.

LLM-based agents increasingly operate under natural-language policy specifications that govern their permitted actions. These specifications inevitably contain ambiguity: a property that has been studied for decades in requirements engineering (RE) and computational linguistics. \citet{berry_kamsties_2004} distinguish between \emph{nocuous} (i.e., harmful) ambiguity, where different stakeholders arrive at different interpretations, and \emph{innocuous} ambiguity, where shared organizational context ensures convergent interpretation. In human RE workflows, most ambiguity is innocuous because developers share domain knowledge, can consult colleagues, and iteratively clarify requirements.

LLM agents lack these disambiguation channels. They cannot consult colleagues, rarely escalate to users, and must resolve ambiguity in real time from the text alone. This raises a question that has not been empirically addressed: does policy ambiguity cause safety violations in LLM agents, and if so, does the \emph{type} of ambiguity predict the severity of the violation? Prior work classifies ambiguity types in policies \citep{drip_r_2026,li_ambiguity_taxonomy_2024} or measures policy understanding quality \citep{policybank_2026}, but no existing study provides a causal, type-granularity mapping from ambiguity to safety violations.

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/fig1_overview.pdf}
\caption{Experimental overview. We construct 300 matched clause pairs across a six-type, two-layer ambiguity taxonomy and inject them into $\tau^2$-bench policy documents. Five models interact with the modified policies across 2,997 episodes. A cross-judge evaluation pipeline classifies violation severity on a four-tier scale.}
\label{fig:overview}
\end{figure*}

We address this gap with a matched-pair causal study on $\tau^2$-bench \citep{tau2_bench_2025}, a dual-control conversational agent benchmark. Using a six-type taxonomy organized into two layers---linguistic and specification, including specification-level gaps that produce equivalent behavioral effects in LLM agents (\S\ref{sec:taxonomy}), we construct 300 ambiguous/unambiguous clause pairs and evaluate five architecturally diverse models across 2,997 episodes.

Our results show that policy ambiguity is consistently nocuous for LLM agents ($+34.0$ pp violation rate increase, conditional OR $= 15.1$), but risk is driven by individual clause wording (22.4\% of variance) rather than type membership (0.4\%). The taxonomy's value lies in predicting failure mechanisms: type membership predicts the dominant mechanism 17$\times$ better than it predicts violation rate ($\text{NMI} = 0.23$ vs.\ $0.01$; Appendix~\ref{app:type_mechanism}), enabling targeted diagnosis (\S\ref{sec:discussion}). Incompleteness is consistently the highest-risk type (46--62\% across all models); the remaining types form a partial ordering with robust extremes but margin-dependent mid-tier structure.

Our contributions are: (1)~the first matched-pair causal study of policy ambiguity's safety impact with perturbation control (\S\ref{sec:method}), observed across five primary models and confirmed in two additional replication models; (2)~a variance decomposition showing that individual clause wording (22.4\%) dominates type membership (0.4\%) and model identity (1.0\%), combined with a mutual information analysis demonstrating that type predicts failure mechanism 17$\times$ better than violation rate (\S\ref{sec:analysis}, \S\ref{sec:discussion}), repositioning the taxonomy from a risk-prediction tool to a diagnostic taxonomy for failure-mechanism prediction; and (3)~an error analysis quantifying \emph{Disambiguation Neglect} in consequential agentic settings---extending LLM ambiguity-collapse findings \citep{liu_ambiguity_2023,hu_ambiguity_2025} to agentic settings---with $\leq$11\% per-model awareness (4.6\% overall), and \emph{systematic nocuity}: 41.3\% of ambiguous clauses trigger majority-convergent violations, indicating clause-level risk factors (\S\ref{sec:analysis}).

\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Experimental Platform}

We conduct all experiments on the original release of $\tau^2$-bench \citep{tau2_bench_2025}, a dual-control conversational agent benchmark spanning retail and airline customer service domains. $\tau^2$-bench provides natural-language policy documents, tool APIs, and multi-turn user interactions with ground-truth evaluation via assertion checking. Its policy injection mechanism allows us to replace individual policy clauses while preserving the surrounding context, enabling controlled ambiguity manipulation.

\subsection{Matched-Pair Design}

For each of the six ambiguity types, we construct 50 \emph{clause pairs}: an ambiguous variant and a matched unambiguous variant that conveys the same intended policy but resolves the ambiguity. Pairs are minimal edits: the unambiguous variant adds only the information necessary to eliminate the target ambiguity (e.g., for scopal ambiguity, replacing ``items may be returned within 30 days'' with ``standard items may be returned within 30 days; customized items are final sale''). This yields 300 clause pairs $\times$ 2 conditions $\times$ 5 models $= 2{,}997$ episodes (3 episodes lost to API errors in one Qwen3 batch).

Clause pairs are generated through a template-based pipeline and reviewed by two independent annotators (both with NLP background) to ensure (1) the ambiguous variant contains exactly one instance of the target type, (2) the unambiguous variant resolves it without changing policy scope, and (3) the ambiguity is plausibly natural. Annotators independently verified type assignments with a 96\% initial agreement (287/300); the 13 disagreements were resolved by discussion, yielding 100\% final consensus. A post-hoc realism validation confirms linguistic consistency with original $\tau^2$-bench policy text (\S\ref{sec:limitations}).

\subsection{Models}

We evaluate five models spanning four providers and two architecture families: GPT-5.4 and GPT-4.1 (OpenAI, proprietary dense), Claude Sonnet 4.6 (Anthropic, proprietary dense), Qwen3-235B (Alibaba, open-source Mixture-of-Experts), and DeepSeek-V3 (DeepSeek, open-source dense). This selection covers frontier and mid-tier proprietary models alongside open-source alternatives with different training philosophies. All API calls use temperature $= 0$, top\_p $= 1$, and max\_tokens $= 4096$, and were made on May 20, 2026.

\subsection{Evaluation}

We use a cross-judge design to mitigate self-evaluation bias: GPT-5.4 judges Claude, GPT-4.1, and Qwen3 traces; GPT-4.1 judges GPT-5.4 and DeepSeek traces. Because our matched-pair design compares ambiguous and unambiguous conditions within the same model--judge pairing, any systematic judge bias cancels in the treatment effect ($\Delta$pp); the cross-judge asymmetry affects only absolute violation rates, not C1 or C2 (\S\ref{sec:limitations}). Judges classify each episode outcome on a four-tier severity scale: none, minor, moderate, and critical. Our primary analysis uses moderate-or-above severity as the violation threshold; minor-only deviations (6.6\% of episodes) are excluded. Including minor violations (overall rate 49.6\% vs.\ 41.0\%) produces even larger effects without changing any directional conclusion.

\paragraph{Cross-judge calibration.} To quantify potential judge bias, we conducted a dual-judge calibration on 100 stratified episodes (60 from GPT-5.4, 40 from other models; 65\% ambiguous).\footnote{The calibration sample stratifies toward ambiguous episodes (65\%) to ensure adequate violation events for $\kappa$ estimation; binary $\kappa$ is condition-agnostic.} Both judges evaluated each episode independently. McNemar's test reveals no systematic detection bias ($p = 0.606$; GPT-4.1-only violations: 9, GPT-5.4-only: 6). Binary agreement is substantial ($\kappa = 0.653$), with weighted four-tier severity agreement reaching $\kappa_w = 0.775$. Per-type binary agreement ranges from $\kappa = 0.385$ (authorization scope) to $0.875$ (incompleteness); severity-level divergence does not affect core conclusions since our primary analyses use binary thresholds (Appendix~\ref{app:severity}).

\paragraph{Human validation.} Two annotators (NLP background), blind to LLM labels and condition, labeled 100 stratified episodes on the four-tier scale. Inter-annotator agreement is substantial ($\kappa = 0.798$, $\kappa_w = 0.762$). Pooled human--LLM agreement ($\kappa = 0.733$, $\kappa_w = 0.807$; per-type breakdown in Appendix~\ref{app:per_type_agreement}) approaches inter-annotator levels, validating the automated pipeline \citep{zheng_llm_judge_2024}. Per-type estimates ($n = 32$--$34$ each) validate the pipeline overall but lack power for type-level comparison.

\subsection{Controls}

\paragraph{Perturbation control.} To confirm specificity to semantic ambiguity, we run 300 episodes with non-ambiguity perturbations (synonym substitution, clause reordering). The perturbation effect is $\Delta_{\text{perturb}} = -0.67$ pp (95\% CI $[-8.0, 6.67]$, containing zero). Even under the worst-case CI bound (6.67 pp), the residual attributable to ambiguity is $\geq$27.3 pp. This control tests surface-level perturbation specificity; the information-addition confound specific to incompleteness is addressed separately via logistic regression (Appendix~\ref{app:confound}).

\paragraph{Reproducibility.} A replication of 30 clause pairs $\times$ 2 models $\times$ 3 replicates yields ICC $= 0.919$ (95\% CI $[0.892, 0.940]$). ICC is computed within the OpenAI family; cross-provider stability is supported by C1 consistency across all seven models.

\subsection{Statistical Analysis}

We use McNemar's test for paired binary outcomes, $\chi^2$ tests with Cram\'{e}r's $V$ for omnibus effects, Fisher's exact test for per-type comparisons, and TOST equivalence testing \citep{lakens_2017} with a primary SESOI of $\pm 15$ pp. We report the matched-pair odds ratio from McNemar's test on discordant outcomes ($n_{\text{disc}} = 580$ of 1{,}497 pairs), which conditions on within-pair disagreement and thus differs from the crude marginal odds ratio. This margin is anchored in a domain-relevant criterion: a type producing $\geq$15 pp more violations warrants distinct policy handling in deployment, and it exceeds the perturbation control bound (6.67\,pp) by $>$2$\times$. We acknowledge margin dependence \citep{anvari_lakens_2021}: 3/6 mid-tier pairs are equivalent at $\pm 10$ pp, all six at $\pm 15$ pp (Table~\ref{tab:tost_sensitivity}). No field-standard SESOI exists for agent policy compliance; $\pm 15$\,pp is a pragmatic threshold exceeding measurement noise by $>$2$\times$. In deployment terms, a 15\,pp violation-rate increase on a policy type would trigger a different audit and remediation strategy, making this margin operationally meaningful. We present full sensitivity across seven margins to enable evaluation at any preferred threshold. We complement TOST with a margin-free bootstrap cluster analysis (Ward's method, $B = 10{,}000$; \S\ref{sec:results}). We apply Holm--Bonferroni correction within three pre-registered test families (Appendix~\ref{app:multiple_comparison}). Post-hoc power exceeds 0.99 for both C1 and C2; TOST power at $\pm 15$ pp is 0.94, dropping to 0.57 at $\pm 10$ pp.

\section{Error Analysis \emph{[Exploratory]}}
\label{sec:analysis}

The following failure mode taxonomy emerges empirically from LLM-coded traces, distinct from the input ambiguity taxonomy (\S\ref{sec:taxonomy}).

We analyze 614 violation traces across all five models, coded into five failure modes (Gwet's AC$_1$ $= 0.62$, $\kappa = 0.52$ \citep{gwet_2014,cohen_1960}). Using LLMs to code LLM failures introduces circularity risk; however, mutual information between type (exogenous) and mechanism labels ($I(\text{type}; \text{mechanism}) = 0.41$ bits, $V = 0.45$; Appendix~\ref{app:type_mechanism}) provides structural evidence against this.

\subsection{Type-Specific Failure Mechanisms}

The five-model data reveal three mechanistically distinct failure patterns that crosscut the specification/linguistic layer boundary.

\emph{Structural misparse}: the agent constructs an incorrect parse and follows it consistently, paralleling garden-path processing \citep{frazier_rayner_1982,ferreira_good_enough_2002} at a behavioral level. Dominates scopal (5/5 models), conditional precedence (4/5), and coreferential (3/5).

\emph{Information gap-filling}: the agent fills missing information with plausible but incorrect defaults. Dominates incompleteness (3/5) and lexical (3/5).

\emph{Boundary violation}: the agent exceeds permission boundaries when authorization scope is underspecified; 69.2\% involve unauthorized escalation with zero conservative refusals ($p < 10^{-4}$, binomial). Dominates authorization scope exclusively (5/5).

Each type's dominant failure mode is majority-consistent ($\geq$3/5 models), with two achieving unanimity (scopal and authorization scope).

\subsection{Disambiguation Neglect}

Agents rarely recognize ambiguity. We code \emph{disambiguation awareness} versus \emph{surface adoption} (proceeding with a single interpretation). Overall awareness is 4.6\% ($\leq$11\% per model); Qwen3 and DeepSeek show zero across all 278 combined traces.

We term this \emph{Disambiguation Neglect}---the absence of disambiguation in default settings, not inability. Prior work shows LLMs collapse ambiguity into single interpretations \citep{liu_ambiguity_2023,hu_ambiguity_2025}; we extend this to agentic contexts where the result is irreversible policy violation. Our mitigation experiment ($-$8.5\,pp; Appendix~\ref{app:mitigation}) confirms disambiguation can be elicited.

\subsection{Systematic Nocuity}

Among ambiguous clauses, 41.3\% trigger majority-convergent violations ($\geq$3/5 models), degrading monotonically across thresholds ($\geq$2/5: 54.7\%, $\geq$4/5: 23.7\%, 5/5: 13.0\%)---a phenomenon we term \emph{systematic nocuity} \citep{berry_kamsties_2004}: models converge on the same incorrect interpretation, invisible in agreement-based evaluation. Of these, 83.9\% share the same dominant failure mode, confirming convergent---not coincident---violations. Incompleteness has the highest universal rate (24\%) and is disproportionately represented (OR $= 2.61$, $p = 0.019$).

